WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.720
The first film showed a channel lowering the toll for leaving water.

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:06.820
A potassium channel goes further.

3
00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:12.560
It passes K⁺ more than a thousand times more readily than Na⁺.

4
00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:15.660
Yet Na⁺ is the smaller ion.

5
00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:19.060
How can a pore turn away the smaller ion?

6
00:00:22.483 --> 00:00:25.983
Look at the narrowest stretch of the pore, the selectivity filter.

7
00:00:27.303 --> 00:00:30.103
It is a short tube lined mostly with oxygens.

8
00:00:31.463 --> 00:00:33.063
Four binding sites sit in a row.

9
00:00:34.363 --> 00:00:36.463
Each site is a cage of eight oxygens.

10
00:00:37.783 --> 00:00:39.683
A sieve this narrow would favour Na⁺.

11
00:00:40.767 --> 00:00:44.527
To enter, K⁺ must shed its water shell and pay the toll.

12
00:00:45.987 --> 00:00:49.327
Inside a site, eight oxygens sit close, where its water sat.

13
00:00:50.587 --> 00:00:52.227
They pay back nearly all of it.

14
00:00:53.307 --> 00:00:55.527
So K⁺ enters at little net cost.

15
00:00:56.617 --> 00:01:00.877
Na⁺ holds its water shell more tightly, so its toll is larger.

16
00:01:02.177 --> 00:01:07.057
In the classic snug-fit picture, Na⁺ is too small to touch all eight oxygens at

17
00:01:07.057 --> 00:01:11.197
once. It sheds its water but is not paid back enough.

18
00:01:12.517 --> 00:01:13.517
So it stalls.

19
00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:15.780
Here is the one idea.

20
00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:19.220
The filter does not sort ions by size.

21
00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:23.100
It sorts them by how much of each toll it pays back.

22
00:01:24.133 --> 00:01:26.253
How exact must that payback be?

23
00:01:27.853 --> 00:01:32.273
Na⁺'s toll is about 70 kJ/mol larger than K⁺'s.

24
00:01:33.233 --> 00:01:36.773
A thousandfold preference needs a gap of only about 18.

25
00:01:37.967 --> 00:01:42.427
So the filter could even give Na⁺ about 52 more than K⁺.

26
00:01:43.767 --> 00:01:46.507
It would still favour K⁺ a thousandfold.

27
00:01:48.087 --> 00:01:51.367
Simulations suggest the oxygens do give Na⁺ more.

28
00:01:52.627 --> 00:01:53.667
Just not enough more.

29
00:01:54.667 --> 00:01:58.767
But a site that repays K⁺ so well should hold on to it.

30
00:01:59.907 --> 00:02:04.047
Yet around a hundred million K⁺ ions can cross each second.

31
00:02:05.227 --> 00:02:06.947
What lets a tightly held ion leave?

32
00:02:12.933 --> 00:02:15.233
The answer is probably crowding.

33
00:02:16.153 --> 00:02:20.593
Several K⁺ ions sit in the filter at once, and like charges repel.

34
00:02:21.873 --> 00:02:26.693
When another ion enters, that repulsion lowers the barrier for the leading ion to

35
00:02:26.693 --> 00:02:29.933
leave. This is the knock-on mechanism.

36
00:02:31.083 --> 00:02:34.443
A Newton's cradle is a fair image, but only an image.

37
00:02:35.703 --> 00:02:37.643
No collision passes energy along.

38
00:02:38.923 --> 00:02:43.763
Whether water molecules separate the ions is still argued, though recent evidence

39
00:02:43.763 --> 00:02:45.243
favours direct contact.

40
00:02:46.367 --> 00:02:48.967
So one short tube is both picky and fast.

41
00:02:50.247 --> 00:02:54.327
It hands K⁺ back almost its whole toll, and Na⁺ too little.

42
00:02:55.627 --> 00:02:57.187
Crowding keeps K⁺ moving.

43
00:02:58.747 --> 00:02:59.987
Size never decided it.

44
00:03:01.067 --> 00:03:01.907
The payback did.
